<h2>The setting</h2>
<p>The plaintiffs sued over advertisements and product detailers in a commercial suit. On 14 May 2026, senior counsel for the defendant companies gave an undertaking, recorded in the order, that there would be no fresh circulation of the impugned material until the next hearing. Alleging breach, the plaintiffs filed a contempt petition — but directed it at the companies&rsquo; directors and authorised representatives, without joining the companies themselves. The respondents met it with a pure point of maintainability.</p>

<h2>The maintainability duel</h2>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old">
    <h3>Respondents&rsquo; objection</h3>
    <p>Section 12(4) and (5) of the Contempt of Courts Act makes officers liable only where the contempt is by a company; without the company on record, the petition cannot proceed. <em>Aneeta Hada v. Godfather Travels</em>, (2012) 5 SCC 661 — holding prosecution of directors under Section 141 NI Act impermissible without the company — supplies the principle, and <em>Kalimirch Vyapari Association</em> (Kerala) and <em>Matrix Cellular Services</em> (Delhi) applied it to contempt.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="arrow">&rarr;</div>
  <div class="col new">
    <h3>Court&rsquo;s answer</h3>
    <p>Non-joinder of the company &ldquo;cannot, by itself, be regarded as fatal to the Petition&rdquo; (para 16). <em>Kalimirch</em> and <em>Matrix Cellular</em> were declared per incuriam (para 26). Contempt jurisdiction under Article 215 is a plenary power of the court to vindicate its own orders; the court may issue notice even on its own motion, and the plaintiffs had in any event offered to implead the companies.</p>
  </div>
</div>
<p>Acting on that view, the court directed that notice issue to the defendants in the suit &ldquo;since the Undertaking had been given on their behalf&rdquo;, and impleaded the two defendant companies as additional respondents (para 28). Replies were directed, vakalatnama defects were ordered cured, and the matter was listed within days (paras 29-32).</p>

<h2>Why the per incuriam declaration matters</h2>
<div class="note">
<p>A single judge ordinarily follows a coordinate bench. The per incuriam route — available where an earlier decision overlooked binding authority or statute — is the exception that lets the court decline to follow it. By taking that route against <em>Matrix Cellular</em>, the court has set up a clear divergence within the Delhi High Court on whether <em>Aneeta Hada</em>&rsquo;s company-first logic governs contempt petitions at all. The question has an obvious structural answer pulling the other way: NI Act prosecution is a creature of statute with the company&rsquo;s offence as its foundation, while contempt is the court&rsquo;s inherent constitutional power to enforce obedience — a power that has never depended on the niceties of party arrays. Expect this issue to travel to a larger bench or the Supreme Court in due course.</p>
</div>

<h2>Practical significance for commercial litigation</h2>
<p>Undertakings are the workhorse of interim arrangements in commercial suits — they avoid injunction findings while freezing conduct. This order strengthens their enforcement in three ways. First, a technically imperfect party array will not buy the alleged contemnor a dismissal; courts will cure the defect by impleadment rather than reward it. Second, officers and representatives who stand behind a corporate undertaking remain personally within the court&rsquo;s reach, since the undertaking was given on the companies&rsquo; behalf by counsel on instructions. Third, speed: the court moved from objection to impleadment to a listed hearing within days, reflecting how seriously breach of a recorded undertaking is taken on the commercial side.</p>

<h2>Takeaways</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li>Draft contempt petitions to join both the company and the responsible officers — this order rescues imperfect arrays, but the safer course is completeness.</li>
  <li>When giving an undertaking in a commercial suit, treat it as an injunction in all but name: circulate it internally, instruct marketing and distribution teams in writing, and document compliance.</li>
  <li>An undertaking recorded in an order binds through Order XXXIX Rule 2A and the Contempt of Courts Act simultaneously; the enforcement menu is the petitioner&rsquo;s choice.</li>
  <li>Maintainability objections resting on coordinate-bench authority can fail where that authority is vulnerable to a per incuriam attack — research the foundations, not just the holding.</li>
</ul>
</div>
